T2  - 2025 PhotonIcs and Electromagnetics Research Symposium - Fall, PIERS-FALL 2025 - Proceedings
AB  - This study introduces an innovative application of a ResNet50-based convolutional neural network (CNN) trained on historical Hainan data. By utilizing the model for automated classification of Equatorial Spread-F (ESF) types in ionograms from Campina Grande, Brazil, during 2024-2025, the results show the pronounced seasonal peak in all ESF occurrences during summer months, with Strong Range Spread-F (SSF) exceeding 17% prevalence, alongside distinct diurnal sequences: RSF and SSF dominate post-sunset, while FSF and MSF peak post-midnight. Temporal shifts in peaks between 2024 and 2025 correlate with electrodynamic processes, particularly post-sunset electric field delays and plasma bubble development via modified Rayleigh-Taylor instability. This approach not only validates CNN-driven automation for large-scale ionogram analysis, but also advances understanding of ESF dynamics in equatorial regions, highlighting longitudinal variations against prior Hainan studies and enabling enhanced space-weather forecasting. © PIERS-FALL 2025.All rights reserved.
