TI  - Characterizing Global Equatorial Ionospheric Irregularities Through SCINDA-GNSS and COSMIC-RO Observations in High-Solar Activity Period
AU  - Seif, Aramesh
AU  - Kumar Panda, Sampad
AU  - Huzaimy Jusoh, Mohamad
AU  - Baily, Scott M.
T2  - 2025 IEEE International Conference on Next-Gen Technologies of Artificial Intelligence and Geoscience Remote Sensing (EarthSense)
AB  - Ionospheric irregularities at the equatorial region, such as plasma bubbles, scintillation, spread-F phenomena and sporadic E layers are some of the most threatening phenomena to radio wave communications and satellite navigation in the world. These broad nighttime disturbances in the ionosphere are dominantly excited by rapidly varying electric fields in the vicinity of dusk, where time-dependent electrodynamic processes generate ionospheric instability, leading to perturbations in electron densities that extend into the equatorward direction. The well-differentiated behaviour of the patterns of such ionospheric irregularities and their seasonal trends is necessary to reduce their adverse impacts on satellite applications that have become so fundamental to technological life on Earth. Significant progress from both ground-based and space-based observations has increased our understanding of the nature of ionization characteristics, their solar cycle variation, and their vulnerability to geomagnetic storms. Here, we address, in a thorough survey, the complex physical processes that produce equatorial ionospheric irregularities. We also investigate the link between the ionospheric scintillation, equatorial plasma bubbles (EPBs), and sporadic E (Es) layers based on the data from 15 SCINDA ground stations and GNSS radio occultation (RO) measurements. SCINDA reports the real-time S4 index, a key parameter for the detection of scintillation. The study reveals that daytime scintillation is associated with the existence of Es layers and nighttime scintillation was mainly caused by the phenomenon of EPBs. These results demonstrate the unique driving characteristics of ionospheric disturbances and their significance to communication and navigation systems.
